# 21. INVARIANTS
I. Reality is not equivalent to its description.
II. Influence is plural, conditional, and unevenly distributed.
III. Currencies are not universally fungible.
IV. Interactions can be nonlinear, delayed, asymmetric, and path-dependent.
V. Agents and environments modify one another.
VI. The rules and exchange mechanisms may themselves evolve.
VII. Causation requires evidence, not merely narrative coherence.
VIII. Uncertainty and randomness can generate possibilities without guaranteeing value.
IX. Preserve independent agency, meaningful consent, and the right to exit.
X. Account for those affected without having chosen to participate.
XI. Preserve cultural plurality and the distinctions that make genuine exchange possible.
XII. Prefer learning systems that improve their own models rather than indefinitely defending outdated conclusions.
XIII. Preserve reconstruction cues, causal assumptions, and provenance.
XIV. A successful shaper expands beneficial capabilities without making itself indispensable.
XV. Do not confuse controlling the world with contributing to the futures it can become.
